# Rails writes a column's reader, an enum's methods and an association's
# into modules the model includes as it is made, so they sit behind the
# class and every module it includes. An `enum` in a concern's `included do`
# lost to the schema's column (`status.upcase` was sent to Integer), and a
# hand-written `def kind` above `enum :kind` lost to the enum, where Ruby
# always runs the `def`. And `self.class` is the class, not Kernel#class's
# `Class`, so `self.class.statuses` finds the enum's class method.
def app.rb:13:12 status=resolved owner=String receiver_type=String
def app.rb:14:16 status=resolved owner=Statusable::ClassMethods defined_via=enum
def app.rb:25:5 status=resolved owner=Gadget site=app.rb:19
